EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-lkshfz_ssh-engine-spike/README.md-4-Compare a minimal, upstream-trackable ReluxNIOSSH fork with libssh2 behind one spike transport contract and select an in-process engine only after one candidate passes the complete Apple-target, host-verification, authentication, direct-tcpip, exec, window, rekey, compatibility, backpressure, cancellation, lifecycle, and memory matrix.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-lkshfz_ssh-engine-spike/README.md-5-
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-lkshfz_ssh-engine-spike/README.md-6-## Scope
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-lkshfz_ssh-engine-spike/README.md:7:In scope: pinned candidate revisions and licenses; iOS and macOS packet-tunnel-compatible static or source integration; raw host-key evidence; Ed25519 and approved fallback authentication; direct-tcpip and bidirectional exec channels; bounded writes; per-channel initial windows and adjustment policy; client byte and time rekey plus server rekey; keepalive, cancellation, metrics, and error mapping; current and supported older OpenSSH profiles plus the real relux server; hundreds of channels; at least 5 GiB mixed traffic; one, two, and four raw SSH connections; loss, reconnect, and descriptor tests; memory comparison; engine ADR and fork maintenance plan. Out of scope: SFTP, interactive shells, agent or X11 forwarding, password prompts, ProxyJump, production lane scheduling or flow migration, QUIC policy implementation, remote relay implementation, and weakening a failed gate to preserve a preferred candidate.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-lkshfz_ssh-engine-spike/README.md-8-
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-lkshfz_ssh-engine-spike/README.md-9-## Acceptance Criteria
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-lkshfz_ssh-engine-spike/README.md:10:1. Both candidates build in the macOS and iOS packet-tunnel target configurations, with pinned revisions, licenses, integration constraints, and reproducible build commands recorded. 2. One common conformance harness obtains raw host-key evidence before acceptance and exercises Ed25519 plus an approved fallback against current OpenSSH, documented older profiles, and the real relux host. 3. Candidate evidence covers independent direct-tcpip and bidirectional long-lived exec channels, early close and half-close, bounded backpressure, cancellation, network loss, reconnect, and no task, channel, socket, or descriptor growth across repeated cycles. 4. Initial receive windows and WINDOW_ADJUST behavior are configurable per channel; tests cover 32 KiB, 64 KiB, and capped bandwidth-delay-product profiles without multiplying a large default by maximum sessions. 5. Client byte and time thresholds and server requests survive at least 5 GiB of simultaneous direct-tcpip and exec traffic, with lane-local failure evidence. 6. Hundreds of concurrent channels and one, two, and four connection trials remain within the extension memory budget and record device, OS, revisions, algorithms, traffic, loss and latency, windows, queued bytes, CPU, physical footprint, peak, and failures. 7. A decision record selects only a candidate that passes every required gate, documents rejected results and residual risks, and routes a red primary candidate to the alternate rather than weakening criteria.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-lkshfz_ssh-engine-spike/TASK-260715-1gjxer_record-m0-ssh-engine-selection/README.md-7-In scope: the approved decision and its rationale; candidate versions and exact dependency pins; adapter boundary; approved algorithms; pre-auth host verification; auth; direct-tcpip; exec and exec/stdin upload; client-rekey trigger; cancellation and lifecycle; bounded-memory posture measured on the macOS harness; license and security-monitoring posture; fork and upstream-rebase owner; the four M3-deferred semantics with their owner task; residual risks; revalidation triggers; downstream readiness for M1 and M2. Out of scope: asserting physical-hardware scale or extension-budget numbers not yet measured, waiving an M0-viability-mandatory gate, reopening the approved decision without new evidence, NIOSSH fork work, lane scheduling, relay protocol, profile UX, SFTP, ProxyJump, and release approval.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-lkshfz_ssh-engine-spike/TASK-260715-1gjxer_record-m0-ssh-engine-selection/README.md-8-
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-lkshfz_ssh-engine-spike/TASK-260715-1gjxer_record-m0-ssh-engine-selection/README.md-9-## Acceptance Criteria
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-lkshfz_ssh-engine-spike/TASK-260715-1gjxer_record-m0-ssh-engine-selection/README.md:10:1. A TASK-ID-scoped ADR maps every M0-viability-mandatory gate to row-level evidence and marks pass, fail, or not run with reason for the selected engine, and preserves the recorded ReluxNIOSSH comparative evidence. 2. The selected engine passes every M0-viability-mandatory gate, including macOS target integration, pre-auth host evidence, approved auth, concurrent direct-tcpip channels achievable on the harness, long-lived exec, client-rekey trigger, real relux-server compatibility, bounded memory, and leak-free lifecycle. 3. A red mandatory gate routes selection back to analysis or to the alternate candidate; no mandatory threshold is weakened after results are known. 4. The ADR records exact dependency pins, adapter boundary, approved algorithms, initial window and rekey policy ranges as injectable values, license notices, security monitoring, and the fork or upstream-rebase owner. 5. The four M3-deferred semantics, the physical scale/memory evidence still owed, rejected evidence, residual risks, revalidation triggers, and the downstream tasks unblocked for M1 and M2 are explicit.
